implementationPlan.description:
ADVERSARIAL SELF-REVIEW, once, as the final step. Load adversarial-self-review.

Read the diff two ways: as repository content trying to get a non-loopback daemon admitted, and as an unlucky Windows or WSL user.
- Can any daemon.json byte produce an admission? Check:
  - an absent, null or array bindHost;
  - a duplicate key, or a key named after the object prototype. Never write that literal: the global rails forbid it.
  - a port that points at another project's daemon or at a non-Moe loopback service;
  - a projectPath field naming another project;
  - a daemonInfoPath outside .moe.
- Can any reply admit without being healthy, this project's and loopback? Check:
  - 'wsl', after task-2f2484b4;
  - case and whitespace variants;
  - an IPv4-mapped address;
  - arrays;
  - a redirect;
  - chunked, slow or oversized bodies;
  - a 503.
- Is every wait bounded by a total deadline? On every path, is every request destroyed and every timer cleared? Does teardown abort the per-forward probe without changing the failure code?
- Is the key never read before admission? Does mcpCaller dial the admitted host?
- Are the refusal codes unchanged, with no new codes? Does no error echo a path, a port or upstream text?
- Is a loopback daemon of this project still admitted: by default, as '127.0.0.1', as '::1', and in another spelling? Does /health keep every field?
- Rails:
  - regression tests are present;
  - no MCP contract change;
  - .moe state stays backward-compatible;
  - no .moe edits and no Git writes;
  - nothing persisted;
  - none of the forbidden patterns;
  - no exploit walkthrough in comments, test names or summaries.
- Would I be embarrassed if this shipped?

Fix any finding and re-run step 6's gate fresh. Then overwrite the Serena memory 'task-task-2cf1c6bee5814ed98eb3d069d97b32b3-handoff' with the landed names (loopbackDaemonHost, MAX_HEALTH_BYTES, the new home of canonicalProjectPath, the /health bindHost field). CQ-R13 and verified-local activation will build on them.
